    │                           ────┬────  
    │                               ╰────── this is a String
    │ 
    │ Note: Matching a Type with only one possible shape has only one outcome.
────╯

[literal-match-shape]
Error: This Case names a value and can still decline it
    ╭─┤ Match.es:52:8 │
    │
 52 │        case 0 where count::isNot(1) { <- "zero" }
    │             ▲       ───────┬───────  
    │             ╰──────────────│───────── this Case names the value
    │                            │         
    │                            ╰───────── this decides after the value already matched
    │ 
    │ Help: Take the Guard off, and ask its question inside the Handler with an 'if'.
    │ 
    │ Note 1: A Match on an Integer takes the VALUE apart: every Case names a value, and the last one answers for every value the Cases above it did not name.
    │ Note 2: So the last Case is reached only by a value none of the Cases above named — and a Guard would let one of them through, which makes that untrue.
────╯

[literal-match-shape]
Error: This Match has no Case for the rest of the values
    ╭─┤ Match.es:58:21 │
    │
 58 │     constant partial = match count -> String {
    │                              ──┬──  
    │                                ╰──── this is an Integer
 59 │        case 0 { <- "zero" }
    │             ▲  
    │             ╰── this Case names a value, not the rest of them
    │ 
    │ Help: Add a 'case _' below, for every value the Cases above miss.
    │ 
    │ Note 1: A Match on an Integer takes the VALUE apart: every Case names a value, and the last one answers for every value the Cases above it did not name.
    │ Note 2: There are more values of Integer than a Match can write down, so the last Case is what makes it answer for all of them.
────╯

─── 4 errors, 1 warning
"
`;

